al analytic on an open set $E$, what must hold at each $a\\in E$?","options":["$f$ is real analytic at $a$.","$f$ is discontinuous at $a$.","$f$ is zero at $a$.","$f$ has no power series near $a$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"d88ff7ccc90603f8820ee64e","time_limit":35,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"no_diagnostic_value"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_real_analytic_function_21857cee_016","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Which situation shows real analyticity at $a=0$?","options":["$f$ is defined only at $0$.","$f(x)=\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}c_nx^n$ on some interval $(-r,r)$.","$f$ agrees with a power series only at $x=0$.","$f$ is not defined near $0$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"85b0a6c7ff0f34b257399bc0","time_limit":30,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"replaced_after_academic_review"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_real_analytic_function_21857cee_017","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"If $f$ and $g$ are real analytic where their sum is defined, what can be said about $f+g$?","options":["It is never differentiable.","It is real analytic.","It is always zero.","It cannot have a power series."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"b8835dc6dd253b49a95ce12f","time_limit":25,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_real_analytic_function_21857cee_018","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"If $f$ is real analytic, which differentiability property follows?","options":["$f$ is differentiable only once, not twice.","$f$ is infinitely differentiable.","$f$ is not continuous.","$f$ has no derivatives."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"0e22aad04306f9beab7ad500","time_limit":25,"curation_status":"retired","retire